This version uses a normal `List<int>`. For each incoming value, binary search finds its sorted position, and `Insert` places it there.

The median is then available at the center of the list. Duplicate numbers can be inserted beside one another without any special handling.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Solution {
    public List<double> GetMedian(int[] arr) {
        List<int> values = new List<int>();
        List<double> result = new List<double>();

        foreach (int number in arr) {
            int left = 0;
            int right = values.Count;

            while (left < right) {
                int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
                if (values[middle] < number) {
                    left = middle + 1;
                } else {
                    right = middle;
                }
            }

            values.Insert(left, number);

            int size = values.Count;
            int center = size / 2;
            if (size % 2 == 1) {
                result.Add(values[center]);
            } else {
                long sum = (long)values[center - 1] + values[center];
                result.Add(sum / 2.0);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

The binary search is `O(log n)`, but moving list elements during `Insert` takes `O(n)`. Across the stream, the worst-case time is `O(n^2)`.

Extra space is `O(n)`. The `long` sum avoids integer overflow when calculating an even-sized median.

A `TreeMap` keeps each different number in sorted order and stores how many times it appears. To get a median, the code walks through the counts until it reaches the needed rank.

This also handles duplicate values correctly. For an even number of values, it finds both middle ranks and averages them.

An empty input returns an empty result, so there is no attempt to read from an empty map.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;

class Solution {
    public List<Double> getMedian(int[] arr) {
        List<Double> result = new ArrayList<>();
        TreeMap<Integer, Integer> counts = new TreeMap<>();
        int total = 0;

        for (int number : arr) {
            counts.put(number, counts.getOrDefault(number, 0) + 1);
            total++;

            if (total % 2 == 1) {
                int middle = valueAtRank(counts, (total + 1) / 2);
                result.add((double) middle);
            } else {
                int left = valueAtRank(counts, total / 2);
                int right = valueAtRank(counts, total / 2 + 1);
                result.add(((double) left + right) / 2.0);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    private int valueAtRank(TreeMap<Integer, Integer> counts, int rank) {
        int seen = 0;

        for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : counts.entrySet()) {
            seen += entry.getValue();
            if (seen >= rank) {
                return entry.getKey();
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }
}
```

Adding a value costs `O(log u)`, where `u` is the number of different values. Finding a rank can visit `O(u)` map entries, so each median update takes `O(u)` time in the worst case.

Space usage is `O(u)` for the frequency map and `O(n)` for the returned medians.

Two heaps keep the middle values ready without sorting the whole stream. The max heap stores the smaller half, and the min heap stores the larger half. The smaller half is allowed to have one extra value.

When a number is added, it first goes into the lower heap. Its largest value is moved to the upper heap, then one value is moved back if the upper heap became larger. This keeps the heap tops at the center.

For example, adding `5, 2, 9` gives medians `5`, `3.5`, and `5`.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
  public:
    vector<double> getMedian(vector<int> &arr) {
        priority_queue<int> lower;
        priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> upper;
        vector<double> result;

        for (int number : arr) {
            lower.push(number);
            upper.push(lower.top());
            lower.pop();

            if (upper.size() > lower.size()) {
                lower.push(upper.top());
                upper.pop();
            }

            if (lower.size() > upper.size()) {
                result.push_back(static_cast<double>(lower.top()));
            } else {
                long long left = lower.top();
                long long right = upper.top();
                result.push_back((left + right) / 2.0);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Each insertion takes `O(log n)` time, while reading the current median takes `O(1)` time.

The two heaps together use `O(n)` space. The `long long` variables keep the average safe when the two integer values are large.

## Intuition
The core idea is to maintain the data in two halves using two heaps: a **Max-Heap** for the smaller half and a **Min-Heap** for the larger half. By balancing these heaps, the median is always easily accessible at the roots. We use a boolean flag to alternate which heap receives the "extra" element, ensuring the size difference between them never exceeds one.



## Approach
1. **Two Heaps:** Initialize a `maxHeap` for the smaller half and a `minHeap` for the larger half. 
2. **Add Numbers:** When adding a number, if the current count is even, we pass the number through the `maxHeap` and move the resulting largest element to the `minHeap`. If odd, we pass it through the `minHeap` and move the smallest element to the `maxHeap`.
3. **Toggle State:** Flip the `even` boolean after every insertion to track whether we have an even or odd number of elements.
4. **Calculate Median:** If the total count is even, the median is the average of both heap tops. If odd, the `minHeap` root contains the middle element.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    private PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(), maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
    private boolean even = true;
    public List<Double> getMedian(int[] nums) {
        // Your code goes here
        List<Double> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        int n=nums.length;
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            addItem(nums[i]);
            ans.add(getMedian());
        }
        return ans;
    }
    private void addItem(int num){
        if(even){
            maxHeap.offer(num);
            minHeap.offer(maxHeap.poll());
        }else{
            minHeap.offer(num);
            maxHeap.offer(minHeap.poll());
        }
        even=!even;
    }
    private double getMedian(){
        if(even){
            return (minHeap.peek()+maxHeap.peek())/2.0;
        }else{
            return minHeap.peek();
        }
    }
}

```

## Complexity
### Time Complexity: 
O(NlogN) For each of the N elements in the input array, we perform offer and poll operations on the priority queues. Each heap operation takes logarithmic time relative to the number of elements in the heap.


### Space Complexity:
O(N) We store all N elements from the input array across the two priority queues to maintain the data structure. Additionally, the result list stores N double values.
